[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Activity and Fragment Lifecycle Under Configuration Changes in Android (Kotlin)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Configuration changes, such as screen rotations, screen size changes, or language switches, cause the Android system to destroy and recreate the current Activity and its associated Fragments by default. Understanding this lifecycle is crucial for preventing memory leaks, retaining UI state, and ensuring smooth user experiences."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Destruction Sequence"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a configuration change occurs, the system tears down the existing Activity. The standard lifecycle callback sequence for an Activity and its Fragment goes from active states down to destruction."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fragment: onPause() -> onStop() -> onDestroyView() -> onDestroy() -> onDetach()"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Activity: onPause() -> onStop() -> onSaveInstanceState() -> onDestroy()"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Re-Creation Sequence"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Immediately after destruction, the system instantiates a brand new Activity and re-attaches the Fragments. Here is the sequence of callbacks during the rebuild process."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Activity: onCreate() -> onStart() -> onRestoreInstanceState() -> onResume()"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fragment: onAttach() -> onCreate() -> onCreateView() -> onViewCreated() -> onStart() -> onResume()"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling State with ViewModel"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent data loss and avoid heavy database or network queries during configuration changes, use the Android Architecture Components ViewModel. ViewModels survive configuration changes automatically."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"class MyViewModel : ViewModel() {
    private val _counter = MutableLiveData<Int>(0)
    val counter: LiveData<Int> get() = _counter

    fun increment() {
        _counter.value = (_counter.value ?: 0) + 1
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Accessing ViewModel in Fragment"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can scope the ViewModel to the Activity or the Fragment. Scoping it to the Activity allows sharing data between multiple Fragments inside the same host Activity."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class MyFragment : Fragment() {
    private val viewModel: MyViewModel by viewModels()

    override fun onViewCreated(view: View, savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onViewCreated(view, savedInstanceState)
        viewModel.counter.observe(viewLifecycleOwner) { count ->
            // Update your UI safely
        }
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for State Preservation"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ViewModel for holding UI-related data that needs to survive configuration changes."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use onSaveInstanceState() only for lightweight transient state, such as scroll positions or text input fields, which need to survive process death."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid storing Context or View references inside a ViewModel to prevent severe memory leaks during rotation."}]
